File Index

/account.test.ts
Purpose: mustChangePassword allowlist enforcement, change-password wrong-current/too-short/success-invalidates-old-session-and-mints-fresh, rotate-master same, email verify wrong-token, email format validation — the latter now a table of 8 rejection cases pinning looksLikeEmail's rules (multi-@, empty local part, dotless/dot-edged domain, whitespace, the 254-char ceiling, and the quadratic-backtracking shape that motivated dropping the regex) plus an acceptance case covering subdomains, plus-tags and apostrophes so the rewrite can't silently get stricter.

/account.ts
Purpose: Session-guarded account actions (change-password, email, email/verify, rotate-master), blocked while mustChangePassword is set except change-password itself; change-password and rotate-master both mint a FRESH session at the new epoch before responding. Email shape checking is looksLikeEmail(), index-scanned rather than regex-matched: the previous /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/ backtracked QUADRATICALLY on failing input because the literal \. is also matched by [^\s@], so every dot position had to be retried as the split point — real CPU per request against the 32kb body cap (CodeQL js/polynomial-redos). Same shape rules as before plus a 254-char RFC 5321 ceiling; still deliberately a shape check, not RFC 5322 validation, since the verification mail is the real proof.

/admin-guard.ts
Purpose: A2 admin guard + server-side strictly-below rules — requireElevated (after sessionGuard) 403s plain 'user' on EVERY admin route incl. list; canManageTier/assignableTiers against the authoritative GLOBAL_TIERS (peers refuse; owner never assignable over HTTP, host scripts only).

/admin.test.ts
Purpose: Plain-user 403 everywhere + no-session 401, manageability flags + no-secret-material regex on the list payload, full strictly-below assignment matrix (peer/upward/self/ceiling refusals, owner unassignable, demotion works, junk id 404), temp-password kills prior session + forces change + audits, search/paging, revoked-credential count drop, services listing, me.globalRole.

/admin.ts
Purpose: A2 admin surface (session + mustChangePassword + requireElevated): GET /api/admin/users paged/searchable w/ LIVE per-account credential counts + manageable flags + the caller's assignableTiers (never hashes/token material); POST :id/role (strictly-below on the target's CURRENT tier AND the new tier); POST :id/temp-password (one-time password shown once, forced change, epoch bump); GET /api/admin/services for the delegation surface. Role changes + temp-passwords append to audit.log. Unknown id 404, known-but-unmanageable 403 (list already reveals existence to elevated actors).

/auth.test.ts
Purpose: Challenge shape, signup success/wrong-answer/duplicate, login success/failure, Phase 15 lockout (429 + retry hint after repeated failures, blocks even the correct password while locked, recovers after natural expiry), me session requirement + mustChangePassword allowlist, logout revocation, forgot-password identical-response invariant, full reset-password flow with no auto-login.

/auth.ts
Purpose: Public-ish flows — anti-bot challenge, signup (one-time password shown once), login (Phase 15: checks loginLockout BEFORE calling authenticate — a locked identifier gets 429 even with the correct password; a failed attempt records a lockout strike, a success clears it)/logout/me, forgot/reset password (anti-enumeration: forgot-password always returns the same response; reset-password never auto-logs-in). A2: the public summary (me/login/signup) carries an additive globalRole so the client can gate its Admin tab.

/device.test.ts
Purpose: Route coverage over real HTTP with a Node-generated P-256 "browser" that signs with ieee-p1363 (the encoding real browsers use — signing the Node DEFAULT way would pass against a wrongly-written server and still break in production). Pins: enrollment returns NO secret (exact key-set assertion), enroll needs a session AND a cleared mustChangePassword, private-JWK and non-P-256 refusals, label cap, nonce replay, nonce burned on a FAILED signature, cross-device nonce, wrong-audience signature failing at verification (proving the audience is signed, not merely sent), DEVICE_REENROLL_REQUIRED after a password change, cross-account revoke 404, revoke-all scoping, and audit lines.

/device.ts
Purpose: AUDIENCE IS NOT CALLER-CHOSEN: enroll resolves the requesting Origin through config's origin->services map and FREEZES the result on the record; assert checks the requested service against that frozen list (403 DEVICE_AUDIENCE_NOT_ALLOWED), so a device enrolled on one app cannot mint a token for another. The origin check is server-side, NOT delegated to CORS — CORS only stops a browser READING a response, while the request still arrives and a non-browser client sends no Origin at all, so an underivable audience is a refusal (403 DEVICE_ORIGIN_REQUIRED / DEVICE_ORIGIN_NOT_CONFIGURED) rather than a default. Check ORDER is load-bearing: nonce -> signature -> audience -> grant, i.e. authenticate THEN authorize, because checking the audience first would answer "what may this device reach" to anyone holding only a deviceId — the same enumeration oracle /challenge is written to avoid. Optional per-service entitlement (deviceGrantRequiredServices) requires an active API key on the account, checked at MINT time so revoking the key lands on the next ~10-minute renewal rather than only at the next enrollment. GET /api/device/origins is PUBLIC (every value is already disclosed to that origin in a CORS header) and exists so auth-client validates ?returnTo= against the SAME list, instead of a second allowlist that would drift. Device-bound credentials — enroll a browser's PUBLIC key, then challenge/assert to trade a signature for a short-lived audience-scoped token. SPLIT MOUNT is the thing to understand: /challenge and /assert are PUBLIC (a deviceId is not a secret, a signature is self-authenticating, and requiring a session to mint a token would defeat the point since the token IS the session equivalent) while enroll/list/revoke sit behind sessionGuard + mustChangePasswordGuard. Getting that backwards either breaks silent re-minting or lets anyone enroll a key against your account. deviceAssertionPayload is LENGTH-PREFIXED, not delimiter-joined, so a field containing the delimiter cannot shift the framing and make two different (deviceId,nonce,service) triples sign identical bytes. The audience is INSIDE the signed bytes, so a signature captured for one service can't mint a token for another. Nonce is consumed BEFORE signature verification so a bad signature still burns it (otherwise a captured nonce is a grinding target). Verification passes dsaEncoding:'ieee-p1363' because WebCrypto emits raw r||s while node defaults to DER — get this wrong and every real browser fails while Node-signed tests still pass. /challenge issues a nonce even for an unknown deviceId, on purpose: otherwise it is a deviceId enumeration oracle. Also owns a hand-rolled CORS middleware (no new dep, same call as session-guard's hand-parsed cookie) mounted ONLY on /api/device so the rest of auth-server's browser-reachable surface cannot widen by accident. It exists because IndexedDB is ORIGIN-scoped: a key enrolled on auth.* is invisible to build.*, so every client origin enrolls its own key and enrollment is inherently a cross-origin CREDENTIALED call. That needs no cookie loosening (hub subdomains are same-SITE, so SameSite=Lax still sends) but does need an exact-match allowlist from DEVICE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS — never a wildcard, which is invalid with credentials and would let a hostile page drive an authenticated enrollment. Vary: Origin is set even on refusal, or a shared cache could serve one origin's allow-header to another. 2026-07-31 (CodeQL): the allowlist is now a normalized Map whose VALUE is echoed into Access-Control-Allow-Origin, never the request's own bytes — the previous code matched on the normalized Origin but echoed the RAW header, so a configured origin reached with trailing slashes would have been answered with an allow-header the browser could not match. Same pass replaced both `/\/+$/` trailing-slash strips (here and in resolveEnrollmentAudience) with a linear backward scan, normalizeOrigin: that regex is quadratic on an Origin of many slashes, and Origin is attacker-controlled on every request.

/introspect.test.ts
Purpose: Missing/bad assertion 401s, valid token/unknown token, expired API key, two-key rotation window, revoked service key, stale/future assertion, replayed nonce, Phase 15 username/expiresAt/tokenType on both an API key (forever, tokenType 'api') and a browser session (real TTL, tokenType 'session').

/introspect.ts
Purpose: Server-to-server ONLY — POST /api/introspect gated by a signed X-Service-Assertion header (never a session cookie); an invalid/missing/unverifiable ASSERTION is 401, an unknown/expired TOKEN being introspected is a normal {valid:false}. Phase 15: a valid response also carries username (fresh account-store lookup), expiresAt, and tokenType ('api'|'session'|'sso'|'obo') — all additive, existing consumers reading only valid/accountId/service/label are unaffected. Phase A adds globalRole ('user' default), same additive rule. 2026-07-31: gained the ONLY rate limit it has ever had (serviceRateLimit before the body parser, chargeService on the verified identity after) — consumers call this at the internal alias AUTH_SERVER_URL, so unlike every browser-facing route group it never crosses an nginx edge zone and had no limiter at either tier.

/keys.test.ts
Purpose: Session/mustChangePassword gating, create+list shape (no token/sha256 leak), past/future expiresAt validation, rotate/revoke including cross-account 404 ownership checks.

/keys.ts
Purpose: Session-guarded API key management (list/create/rotate/revoke), blocked entirely during mustChangePassword; mintApiKey reads the account's CURRENT epoch fresh per request rather than trusting the session.

/resolve-username.test.ts
Purpose: Missing/bad assertion 401s, a real username resolves to {found:true,id,username}, an unknown one returns {found:false} not an error, case-insensitive match (canonical stored casing returned, not the query's), response shape pinned to exactly {found,id,username} — no other account fields leak.

/resolve-username.ts
Purpose: Server-to-server ONLY (2026-08-16), same assertion/rate-limit shape as introspect.ts — POST /api/service/resolve-username lets a registered service turn a memorable USERNAME into the opaque accountId it actually needs, so consumers stop requiring an operator to paste a raw id directly (mud-builder-server's role grants used to, and a real user typo'd their username where the id belonged — see apps/mud-builder-server/src/routes/roles.ts's own note). Any registered service may resolve ANY username — no per-caller privilege distinction, since the privilege decision belongs to the CALLING service (e.g. mud-builder's canGrant()); usernames aren't secret here already (signup uniqueness reveals them immediately). Deliberately returns only {found,id,username}, never other account fields.

/safe.ts
Purpose: Shared try/catch route wrapper — maps a thrown AuthError to its HTTP status, anything else to a 500; spreads AuthError's optional `code` into the body for failures clients must branch on.

/service-rate-limit.ts
Purpose: The ONLY rate limiter on the two server-to-server route groups (/api/introspect, /api/token-exchange). Exists because the nginx edge zones only charge traffic that reaches nginx, and consumer services call auth-server at its internal alias (AUTH_SERVER_URL = auth-server.shatteredarchive.dev:62000), so those calls cross the compose network and never transit the edge — the "reaches the container directly" case rate-limit.ts was written for. Every OTHER route group here is browser-facing and IS edge-limited, which is why this deliberately does not generalize into app-wide middleware. Two keyings mirroring DeviceRateLimiters: serviceRateLimit (per-IP) mounts BEFORE the body parser so an unauthenticated flood buys neither a JSON parse nor an Ed25519 verification; chargeService (per-service) is called AFTER the assertion verifies, keyed on the proven identity — the key that survives a caller being rescheduled onto a new container IP or scaled to replicas, and the only one that bounds a single compromised service key. Sizing is a runaway ceiling, NOT traffic shaping: introspect sits on the hot path of every authenticated request to every consumer service, so tripping this means something is stuck or stolen, not merely busy.

/session-guard.ts
Purpose: Cookie -> keyStore.verify() session guard, plus mustChangePasswordGuard enforcing the Constraints allowlist (GET /api/auth/me, POST /api/account/change-password, POST /api/auth/logout stay reachable); builds its allowlist key from req.originalUrl, NOT req.path — req.path is mount-relative inside an app.use(path, ...)-registered middleware, a real bug this app hit and fixed during manual smoke-testing.

/sso.test.ts
Purpose: Approve happy path, 401 without a session, MUST_CHANGE_PASSWORD block, identical generic 400 for unknown-service vs wrong-URI, all-keys-revoked service refusal.

/sso.ts
Purpose: Browser half of the Phase A SSO hand-off — POST /api/sso/approve (session + mustChangePassword guarded): a logged-in user consents to a consumer service, minting a one-time 60s code its BACKEND redeems at /api/token-exchange; unknown-service and unregistered-redirect-URI failures share ONE generic 400 (registration state is not a public oracle). 2026-08-01 adds GET /api/sso/validate, same guards and same generic 400, answering "is this (service, redirect_uri) pair sanctioned?" BEFORE the consent screen renders. approve alone was not enough: it refuses to mint a code for an unregistered URI, but by then the browser is already on a consent page built from attacker-supplied values — and that page's Cancel button navigates to redirect_uri to report access_denied WITHOUT asking the server anything, which made the hub an open redirect via the DENY path (approve was never exploitable). It also stopped "Continue to &lt;attacker text&gt;?" being rendered on the real login domain at all. Adds no oracle: it reveals nothing approve did not already reveal to the same authenticated caller, and it sits behind the session guard so an anonymous visitor cannot probe the registry.

/test-helpers.ts
Purpose: Shared route-test scaffolding (not a *.test.ts itself) — starts a real Express server on a random port against a tmp data dir with a seeded 3-question pool, a recording test mailer, and signup/login/cookie helpers; challengeThrottle/loginLockout are both constructed effectively-unthrottled by default, override harness.deps.loginLockout per-test (same object reference registerRoutes closed over) to exercise real lockout behavior.

/token-exchange.test.ts
Purpose: Full approve→code→exchange→introspect round trip incl. globalRole, code replay 400, cross-service redeem refusal + code burn, wrong redirectUri, no-assertion/cookie-instead 401s, unknown grantType, OBO happy path + foreign-audience rejection + chain refusal + different-registered-target rule.

/token-exchange.ts
Purpose: Server-to-server ONLY (assertion-gated like introspect) — the single mint point for consumer bearer tokens, enforcing Phase A audience rules: authorization_code redeems only for the ASSERTING service (7d 'sso' token), on_behalf_of accepts only a caller-audience subject token of kind api/sso (2min 'obo' token for a DIFFERENT registered service) — 'session' and 'obo' subjects refused so hub sessions never leave and OBO can never chain. Trust-path failures stay generic. 2026-07-31: rate-limited like introspect and for the same reason (reached at the internal alias, never via the edge) — serviceRateLimit before the parser, chargeService on the proven caller before any code redemption or minting.

/validation.ts
Purpose: Request-body validators shared across route modules — requireString, strict requireNewUsername (signup only), requireNewPassword (12-char minimum), requireChallengeAnswers, requireOptionalExpiresAt (null/absent = forever, otherwise must be a real future date).
